{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790966809,"nonce":"n1V6bIb+66zpXrKJdl66oHOufoU=","title":"zyn_peg's first-byte alternative guard (c367b89) makes parse errors stop naming the","message":"zyntax `c367b89` — \"perf(zyn_peg): decide an alternative on the byte\nahead of it\" — halves parse time by guarding each alternative against\nthe byte ahead and skipping the ones that byte rules out. Parsing is\nunaffected: the analysis is conservative, so a guard never skips an\nalternative that could have matched.\n\n**Diagnostics are.** An alternative that is never tried records no\nexpectation, so the failure position no longer knows about it. For\n`signal bad: = 3` the parser sits at the type position, every concrete\ntype alternative starts with a letter, the byte ahead is `=`, and all of\nthem are guarded out — the error degrades from naming `i32` and friends\nto \"expected ASCII_ALPHA\", plus \"1 other alternative was possible\".\n\nCaught by `parse_diagnostics::a_parse_error_renders_the_offending_line`,\nwhich asserts the alternatives are named. Bisected across the nine\ncommits between `318a8e5` and `96983e2`: good at `cc924bb` and\n`c8c02a9`, bad at `c367b89`.\n\nRESOLVED upstream by `67a8bff` — \"a skipped alternative still says what\nit expected\" — which records the skipped alternative's expectation\nanyway, so the speed no longer costs the message. Blinc pins\n`ff39274` or later.\n\nThe shape of the bug is worth keeping: a perf change that only skips\nwork provably doomed can still be a behaviour change, because the work\nit skips was also producing information. Diagnostics are the usual\ncasualty, and no correctness test catches it.","files":null}]}